<a name="oracledateformat"/><h1>Date Format</h1>

<p>Oracle clients expect DATE columns to include the time of day, but an Oracle back-end's default date format usually drops it.  To keep it, set the <i>date_to_text_format</i> parameter in the connection tag's connect string (see the <a href="../configreference.html#oracle">Configuration Reference</a>), for example:</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><font color="#990000">&lt;</font><font color="#008080">connection</font> string<font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"user=exampleuser;password=examplepassword;oracle_sid=ora1;date_to_text_format=YYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI:SS"</font><font color="#990000">/&gt;</font></tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<p>DATE columns are then returned in that format.  TIMESTAMP columns, and how dates in queries are parsed, are unaffected.  Use a numeric, fixed-width format, with only /, - and : as delimiters, like the one above, or the date may not be parsed correctly.</p>

